            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                PD-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                \-algebra
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Some sources include
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_0
              in the data rather than as convention. Some sources give the data as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_n : I \to A
              typing while including
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_n(x) \in I
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
              as an axiom.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Genuine powers can be constructed in the expected way from the divided powers, and when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is torsion free, the reverse is true:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,I,\gamma)
              is a divided power algebra, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo !
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n! \gamma_n(x) = x^n
              for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in I
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
              (taking
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^0:=1
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              It is true for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=0
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
              by definition. For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo !
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mn 1
                      %mo !
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n! \gamma_n(x) = (n-1)! \gamma_{n-1}(x) \cdot 1! \gamma_1(x) = x^{n-1} \cdot x
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a commutative, torsion free ring with an ideal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n
              is an
              = succeed "-th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n!)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in I
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,I)
              has a unique divided power structure, and it is given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_n(x) = x^n / n!
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              The hypotheses imply the quotients
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n / n!
              are unique and well-defined, and any divided power structure on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,I)
              must be given by that formula. It’s straightforward to check the definition does give a divided power algebra.
          %p So in the torsion free case, the divided power algebras are precisely of the motivating form. In positive characteristic, though, examples can be somewhat more exotic.
          %h2#categorically Categorically
          %p We can define the concept of divided power in any symmetric monoidal category.
